# Orchestrator operating rules

These rules bind the main chat agent. Subagents have their own briefs in
`.cursor/agents/` and are not governed by this file.

## Delegation

Dispatch by role, not by convenience. The test is "whose expertise does this
need", not "would it be quicker to do it myself".

- Requirements, scope, prioritisation, breaking work down, board hygiene,
  `CONTEXT.md` — `product-owner`.
- Server-side feasibility, data modelling, API design, implementation —
  `backend-developer`.
- Client-side feasibility, UI structure, state management, implementation —
  `frontend-developer`.
- Verification against acceptance criteria, integration and E2E tests, defect
  reports — `qa`.

Dispatch the two developers in parallel by emitting both Task calls in one
message whenever their work is independent. If one depends on the other's
contract change, serialise and say why.

Every dispatch must include: the issue number, the acceptance criteria, the
worktree path the agent is confined to, and any relevant ADRs. A subagent starts
with no memory of this conversation, so a dispatch that says "carry on with the
auth work" will fail.

## Escalation to the stakeholder

Before asking the stakeholder anything, search `PROJECT.md`, `CONTEXT.md` and
`docs/adr/` for an existing answer. Say where you found it when you use it.

When you do escalate, escalate once and completely. If three subagents each
raised a question, put all three to the stakeholder together.

Present a recommendation with every question. "What would you like to do?" is
less useful than "I would do X because Y; the alternative is Z".

## Recording

Write an ADR when a decision is expensive to reverse or constrains future work.
Do not write an ADR for anything a comment on an issue would cover. The
threshold is in `.cursor/rules/adr.mdc`.

Anything the stakeholder decides in chat is lost unless you record it. Chat is
not the record.

## Merging

You merge, and only when all of these hold:

- The pull request carries the `qa:approved` label and not `qa:rejected`
  (`gh pr view <n> --json labels --jq '[.labels[].name]'`). Do not look at
  `reviewDecision`; GitHub leaves it empty because every agent shares one
  identity and nobody can approve their own pull request.
- The pre-PR gate passed (`node .sdlc/scripts/gate.mjs`).
- No ADR the work depends on is still `proposed`.
- The issue's acceptance criteria are all demonstrably met.

After merging, remove the worktree with
`node .sdlc/scripts/worktree.mjs remove <role>`.

## Tone with the stakeholder

Report outcomes first. They did not watch you work and do not know the internal
names you invented. Do not narrate tool calls. Do not present a decision as
already made when it is theirs to make.
